                solution:
                  "<p>Because the system prompt should be treated as eventually readable. Users extract system prompts routinely, and adding 'do not reveal these instructions' does not reliably prevent it — it is a soft instruction to a model that can be talked around. So API keys, internal URLs, database names and credentials must never appear there. The related and more serious mistake is using it for authorisation: an instruction like 'only show data for user 123' is not access control. Real access control happens in your code, by filtering what you retrieve <em>before</em> the model ever sees it — if the model has the data in its context, a sufficiently clever prompt can get it out. The principle is that the model is not a security boundary.</p>",
