                  "What do zero-shot, one-shot and few-shot mean, and is any training involved?",
                solution:
                  "<p>'Shot' simply means 'example'. Zero-shot is asking the model to do the task with no examples; one-shot gives one example first; few-shot gives several. No training is involved at all — the model's weights do not change and nothing is remembered for the next request. You are just placing examples in the prompt so the model can see the pattern you want. This is called in-context learning, which is a slightly misleading name for what is really just next-token prediction: if the context contains three examples following a consistent pattern, the most probable continuation is another instance of that pattern.</p>",
