                question:
                  "How do you make an LLM reliably return JSON your code can parse?",
                solution: `<p>There are three levels, and knowing which one you are on is the point:</p>
             <ol>
               <li><strong>Ask nicely</strong> — 'respond with only JSON'. Works most of the time, fails unpredictably. It is a request, not a guarantee.</li>
               <li><strong>Shape the generation</strong> — assistant prefill so the model starts mid-object and cannot write a preamble, stop sequences, few-shot examples of the exact format. More reliable, still not guaranteed.</li>
               <li><strong>Constrained decoding</strong> — the provider's structured-output or tool-calling mode with a JSON Schema. This is different in kind: the constraint is applied <em>at the token level during generation</em>, masking out any token that would make the output invalid, so invalid JSON is structurally impossible rather than merely unlikely.</li>
             </ol>
             <p>The key idea to convey is that Level 3 works by masking tokens rather than by asking politely. Anything a user depends on should be on Level 3.</p>`,
