                  "Explain the trap where a required field causes hallucination.",
                solution: `<p>If your schema declares <code>invoiceNumber</code> as a required string with no null option, and the invoice is smudged so the number is unreadable, the model has no legal way to express that. It cannot omit the field — the schema forbids it. It cannot return null — the type forbids it. So it invents one: correctly formatted, completely fabricated. Your validator passes it, because it <em>is</em> a valid string.</p>
             <p>You have built the hallucination into the schema. The fix is to give every field an escape hatch — a nullable type with a description saying 'null if not clearly legible, do not guess' — and then handle null in your code by routing to human review. A useful addition is an explicit <code>unreadableFields</code> array, which turns the model's uncertainty into structured data you can act on rather than something you have to infer.</p>`,
