                question:
                  "Does schema validation mean the output is correct?",
                solution:
                  "<p>No — it means the output has the right <em>shape</em>, not the right <em>values</em>. A perfectly schema-valid object can contain an invoice total that does not match its line items, a date in 1823, a customer ID that exists nowhere in your database, or a fabricated invoice number. Schema validation is the first and cheapest layer of checking, and it eliminates a whole class of crashes, but semantic and business-rule validation are separate layers you still have to write: do the numbers reconcile, is the date plausible, does the referenced entity exist. Treating schema validity as correctness is one of the most common ways teams convince themselves an extraction pipeline is reliable when it is not.</p>",
