                  "Why does field order matter in a JSON schema?",
                solution:
                  "<p>Because generation is strictly left to right, so field order determines what exists before what. If you put <code>answer</code> before <code>reasoning</code>, the model commits to the answer with no working room and the reasoning becomes rationalisation for a decision already made — you pay the token cost of chain-of-thought and get none of the accuracy benefit. Putting <code>reasoning</code> first means the answer token is genuinely conditioned on the reasoning tokens. So in a schema, field order is a correctness issue rather than a style preference. It is worth calling out because the broken version looks entirely reasonable in code review: both fields are present, everything validates, and the improvement silently never arrives.</p>",
