                question:
                  "How does structured output relate to tool and function calling?",
                solution:
                  "<p>They are the same mechanism. When you define a tool, you supply a JSON Schema for its arguments, and the model produces arguments conforming to it through the same constrained-decoding machinery. So every lesson here transfers directly to tool design: use enums for arguments with a known set of valid values, make arguments nullable where the value may genuinely be unknown so the model is not forced to invent one, write clear descriptions because the model reads them when deciding what to pass, and keep schemas flat rather than deeply nested. On many providers, tool-calling mode is also the most reliable route to structured output generally — you define a single tool that represents your output shape and force the model to call it.</p>",
