                  "Why can a complex nested schema reduce answer quality?",
                solution:
                  "<p>Because the model is spending capacity on producing structure rather than on getting the content right, and because constrained decoding narrows its choices at every step in ways that interact badly with deep nesting. Deeply nested objects, long arrays of objects, and schemas with dozens of fields all measurably reduce accuracy, and they lengthen output, which increases both cost and the chance of hitting the token limit mid-object. The practical guidance is to prefer flat schemas, and to split a large extraction into two or three focused calls rather than one enormous one — which usually improves accuracy <em>and</em> makes failures easier to isolate, since you learn which part failed rather than getting one unusable object.</p>",
