                solution: `<p>Before blaming the model, check what the model actually received — log the fully rendered prompt for a failing request. Then look for:</p>
             <ol>
               <li><strong>A variable rendering empty or as 'undefined'</strong>, producing a fluent answer about nothing.</li>
               <li><strong>Truncation</strong> — a variable larger in production than in your test fixtures, silently cut off or pushing the request against the context limit.</li>
               <li><strong>Whitespace or indentation differences</strong> introduced by a reformat, or a trailing space at the end of the prompt.</li>
               <li><strong>A conditional branch</strong> that testing never exercised, producing a prompt nobody has read.</li>
               <li><strong>Injection</strong> — user content escaping its fence and being read as instructions.</li>
               <li><strong>A fragment that stopped being included</strong> after a refactor, removing a rule you assumed was present.</li>
             </ol>
             <p>The general lesson is that logging the rendered prompt (with an appropriate redaction policy for personal data) turns this from guesswork into a two-minute check.</p>`,
