                  "Why do you need output validation at all if you are using structured output mode?",
                solution:
                  "<p>Because constrained decoding guarantees the <em>shape</em>, not the <em>values</em>. A schema-valid object can contain an invoice whose line items sum to 4,000 while the total field says 400,000, a date in 1823, a customer ID that exists nowhere in your database, or a completely fabricated invoice number that happens to be a valid string. <strong>Most real extraction errors are schema-valid.</strong> So schema validation is the second of five layers, not the whole story — you still need business-rule checks, which are ordinary deterministic code you already know how to write, cost nothing, and catch exactly the errors schema validation cannot see. Treating schema validity as correctness is one of the most common ways teams convince themselves a pipeline is reliable when it is not.</p>",
