                solution: `<p>They have different triggers, different strategies, and mixing them produces a retry loop that burns money and fixes nothing.</p>
             <p><strong>Transport retries</strong> are for when the request never really happened — a 429 rate limit, a 5xx, a network timeout. The correct strategy is exponential backoff with jitter, resending the identical prompt. Jitter is essential: without it, every client that received a 429 retries at the same instant and you have built your own thundering herd. Respect <code>Retry-After</code> headers when the provider sends them.</p>
             <p><strong>Content retries</strong> are for when the call succeeded but the answer was bad — invalid JSON, schema violation, failed business rule. Here you must <em>not</em> simply resend the same prompt, because at temperature 0 you will get the identical bad answer and pay for it twice. You have to change something: feed the validation error back, nudge the temperature, simplify the schema, or escalate to a stronger model.</p>`,
