                question:
                  "What is the difference between prompt caching and semantic caching?",
                solution:
                  "<p>They are completely different techniques with completely different risk profiles. <strong>Prompt caching</strong> reuses the <em>prefill computation</em> for a stable prompt prefix; the model still runs and generates a fresh answer, so output quality is entirely unaffected and there is no correctness risk at all. <strong>Semantic caching</strong> returns a <em>previously generated answer</em> for a semantically similar question, skipping the model entirely — which saves far more but carries real risk: the cached answer may be stale, or the new question may be similar enough to match but different enough that the answer is wrong. Prompt caching is safe to enable almost everywhere; semantic caching needs careful thought about invalidation and about how close is close enough. Interviewers sometimes conflate them, so separating them cleanly is a good signal.</p>",
