                question:
                  "A user says the assistant gave a wrong answer. Where do you look first in a RAG system?",
                solution:
                  "<p>At retrieval, not generation. In a RAG system, <strong>most apparent hallucinations are retrieval failures wearing a generation costume</strong>. Pull the logged chunks for that request and read them: very often the answer simply was not in what you passed, and the model did what it was told — answered from the provided documents. Specific things to check: was the right chunk retrieved at all (measure recall@k on a golden set); was it retrieved but ranked below your cut-off (a reranking problem); were two contradictory versions retrieved, leaving the model to silently pick one; did the answer span a chunk boundary so neither chunk contained it whole; or was a metadata filter excluding the right document. Only after ruling all of that out do you look at the generation prompt. Instrumenting the two halves separately is what makes this a five-minute investigation rather than a week of guessing.</p>",
