                question:
                  "Why does RAG need hybrid search rather than just semantic search?",
                solution:
                  "<p>Because embeddings match <em>meaning</em>, and an exact identifier has very little meaning to match. A query containing 'error ERR-4471B' embeds to roughly 'an error occurred', so semantic search returns documents about errors in general and very likely misses the one document containing that exact code. Meanwhile keyword search finds it instantly but fails on 'why did my payment bounce', where there is no keyword overlap with the relevant document. So you run both and fuse the results — usually with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, which combines by rank rather than score precisely because BM25 scores and cosine similarities live on incomparable scales. Anything with product codes, error codes, case numbers, rare proper nouns or version strings needs this, which in practice is most enterprise corpora.</p>",
