                  "<p>Keep a table <strong>whole</strong> as a single chunk wherever possible, because a table's meaning lives in the relationship between its header row and its cells, and splitting destroys that. If a table is genuinely too large for one chunk, split by rows and <strong>repeat the header row in every piece</strong>, otherwise the fragments are just unlabelled numbers. Render it as a Markdown or HTML table rather than flattened text, so it survives into the prompt and the model can actually read it. And add a caption describing what the table shows — its subject, the units, the time period — so it is retrievable by meaning as well as by the digits it contains, since a user asking 'what were Q3 revenues by region' shares no vocabulary with a grid of numbers. For very large tabular data, the honest answer is often that it should be a database query rather than a retrieval target at all.</p>",
