                solution:
                  "<p>Semantic chunking embeds each sentence, measures similarity between consecutive sentences, and places a boundary where similarity <em>drops</em> — the point where the topic shifts. It produces boundaries that follow meaning rather than formatting, which is genuinely valuable for unstructured prose. The cost is an embedding call per sentence at index time, which is real at corpus scale, plus the added pipeline complexity of tuning the drop threshold. It is worth it for content with no usable structure — call transcripts, meeting notes, long unformatted prose. It is usually <em>not</em> worth it for structured documents, where heading-based chunking gives you better boundaries for free, since the author's headings encode topic shifts more reliably than sentence similarity infers them. The honest framing is that semantic chunking is a fallback for structureless content rather than a general upgrade.</p>",
