                  "<p>Because the vector is not reversible — you cannot recover the text from the embedding, and you need the text twice on every query: to build the prompt and to show the user for citation. Storing it denormalised alongside the vector means one lookup instead of two, which removes a network round trip from the hot path. The alternative, keeping only IDs and fetching text from a separate store, adds latency to every request for no benefit unless the text is very large. Two caveats worth mentioning: vector stores impose per-record metadata size limits, so extremely large chunks may not fit and need the two-store pattern; and this text is a second copy of potentially sensitive content, so it falls in scope for your classification, encryption and deletion policies exactly like the source document.</p>",
