                  "Explain HNSW and what its parameters do.",
                solution: `<p>HNSW builds a multi-layer proximity graph. Upper layers are sparse and used for long-range jumps across the vector space; lower layers are dense and used for fine local search. A query enters at the top, greedily moves toward closer neighbours, descends a layer, and repeats until it converges.</p>
             <ul>
               <li><strong>M</strong> — connections per node. Higher gives better recall at the cost of memory and build time. Build-time only.</li>
               <li><strong>efConstruction</strong> — how broadly the algorithm searches while <em>building</em> the graph. Higher builds a better index, more slowly. Build-time only.</li>
               <li><strong>efSearch</strong> — how broadly it searches at <em>query</em> time. This is the runtime dial you turn when recall is too low.</li>
             </ul>
             <p>Its strengths are an excellent recall/latency trade-off and support for incremental inserts, which matters for a live corpus. Its main weakness is memory: the graph itself roughly doubles the footprint beyond the raw vectors, which is why very large corpora often move to IVF with product quantization or a disk-based index.</p>`,
