                solution:
                  "<p>IVF clusters the vectors with k-means at build time, then at query time finds the nearest few cluster centroids and searches only inside those clusters. Its parameters are <code>nlist</code>, the number of clusters, and <code>nprobe</code>, how many are searched per query — the runtime recall dial. Compared with HNSW it is substantially more memory-efficient and pairs naturally with product quantization for heavy compression, but it requires a training pass over representative data before indexing and handles frequent updates less gracefully. Its characteristic failure mode is a <strong>boundary miss</strong>: if the true nearest neighbour happens to sit just across a cluster boundary from the query, and that cluster is not among the ones probed, it is invisible regardless of how similar it is. Raising <code>nprobe</code> mitigates it at the cost of latency, which is exactly the same recall/speed trade in a different shape.</p>",
