                  "<p>Because the ANN structure was built over the entire vector space with no knowledge of your filter. When the filter is highly selective — say it matches 0.1% of the corpus — the graph traversal has to explore far more nodes before accumulating k results that satisfy it, so latency rises sharply. Some engines respond by falling back to brute-force scanning of the filtered subset, which may or may not be faster depending on how large that subset is. Different vector databases handle this with very different quality, using pre-filtering, filtered traversal or partitioned indexes. The practical guidance is to test filtered queries at production scale and realistic selectivity rather than benchmarking unfiltered, and where a filter value is both common and important — a tenant ID — to partition into separate namespaces instead of filtering, since searching within a small partition sidesteps the problem entirely.</p>",
