                  "<p>Usually very little — typically 20 to 80 milliseconds against a total of one and a half to five seconds, where generation dominates at one to four seconds. That matters because it tells you where <em>not</em> to optimise: tuning the index when generation is the bottleneck is effort spent on the wrong stage. The correct approach is to instrument per stage — query rewriting, retrieval, reranking, context assembly, generation, verification — and attack whatever actually dominates. In most systems the biggest wins are streaming (so time-to-first-token is what users feel), shortening the prompt via reranking, and prompt caching. Vector search becomes worth optimising when you see it exceed its budget, which usually happens because of a selective filter, an oversized <code>efSearch</code>, or an index that has outgrown its memory and started paging.</p>",
