                  "<p>Yes, and nothing surfaces it automatically. An HNSW graph grown incrementally over a year is not the graph you would build from scratch over the same corpus — connectivity degrades, particularly if the distribution of your content has shifted, and accumulated tombstones from deleted vectors make traversal less efficient. Latency also creeps up at fixed parameters as the corpus grows, and an <code>efSearch</code> tuned at one million vectors may be wrong at ten million. The practices that address it: re-measure recall periodically against a flat baseline on a sample rather than assuming stability; schedule compaction to reclaim tombstoned space; rebuild the index from stored vectors occasionally, which is far cheaper than re-embedding since you already have the vectors; and revisit the runtime dial as the index grows. Dashboarding recall alongside index size makes the drift visible before users report it.</p>",
