                  "<p>Learned sparse models such as SPLADE produce a sparse, vocabulary-sized vector like BM25, but the term weights are <em>learned</em> by a transformer rather than computed statistically, and the model performs <strong>term expansion</strong> — a document about 'automobile' gets non-zero weight on 'car' as well. So you get exact-match behaviour and some semantic matching in a single representation, searchable with standard inverted-index infrastructure rather than a separate vector store. It sits genuinely between BM25 and dense embeddings. The costs are a heavier indexing step, since every document needs a model pass, and a larger index than plain BM25 because of the expanded terms. It is worth naming in an interview to show depth, with the honest caveat that hybrid BM25-plus-dense remains the more common production choice because it is simpler to operate and easier to reason about.</p>",
