                  "<p>They complicate the sparse side considerably more than the dense side. BM25 depends on tokenisation, stemming and stop-word lists, all of which are language-specific — you need the right analyser per language, and languages without whitespace word boundaries such as Japanese, Chinese and Thai need dedicated segmentation before BM25 means anything. Cross-lingual matching is essentially impossible on the sparse side: a query in Hindi will not match an English document by keyword. The dense side handles cross-lingual retrieval if you use a genuinely multilingual embedding model, which is the main argument for weighting it higher on non-English traffic. Practical approaches: detect language at both index and query time and route to the correct analyser; keep separate indexes or fields per language; and consider translating the query before the sparse search when documents are predominantly in one language. And evaluate per language, because an English-only golden set will hide all of this.</p>",
