  <ul style="margin-top: 1.0rem; margin-left: 1.5rem; list-style-type: disc;">
    <li style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Source coverage</strong> — for comparative or "what do we say about X across the business" questions, deliberately include at least one chunk from each distinct document rather than eight from the best one.</li>
    <li style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Recency</strong> — when two chunks say similar things, prefer the newer, and consider dropping a chunk that is superseded rather than letting the model see both.</li>
    <li style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Authority</strong> — an approved policy outranks a draft or a meeting note on the same topic.</li>
    <li style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Completeness</strong> — a chunk that ends mid-sentence is worth expanding to its parent section rather than passing as a fragment.</li>
    <li style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Cache alignment</strong> — keep the stable prefix (system prompt, tools, few-shot) first and put retrieved content after the cache breakpoint, so prompt caching still works.</li>
  </ul>

