                  "What memory does an agent need, and how do you implement it beyond stuffing history into the prompt?",
                solution: `<p>Everything starts from the model being <strong>stateless</strong> — all memory is me putting something back into the prompt. And short-term and long-term are <em>different engineering problems</em>: short-term is a <strong>compression</strong> problem bounded by tokens; long-term is a <strong>retrieval</strong> problem bounded by relevance, which makes it RAG over your own history with all the same failure modes.</p>
             <p>Short-term I solve with a rolling summary plus recent turns verbatim, and specifically <strong>structured state rather than prose</strong> — a small JSON object holding the goal, confirmed constraints, decisions and open questions. It is compact and it does not drift the way repeated re-summarisation does. In an agent, tool result compression matters more than dialogue compression.</p>
             <p>Long-term has a write path and a read path, and the write path is the hard half people skip: save <em>durable facts, not conversation</em>, default to saving almost nothing, and retrieve five to ten at the start of a turn.</p>`,
