                  "<p>If you appended blindly, both get retrieved and the model contradicts itself confidently — February says they work at Acme, August says Globex. The fix is structural rather than a retrieval-time patch. <strong>Timestamp everything</strong>: without a written-at date you cannot resolve anything. Store memories with a <em>subject and predicate</em>, not just free text, so on write you can find an existing memory about the same attribute. Then <strong>supersede rather than append</strong> — mark the old one superseded with a pointer to the new value rather than deleting it, which keeps an audit trail and lets you roll back a bad extraction. On retrieval, filter out superseded rows entirely. And when two live memories genuinely disagree, <strong>surface the as-of date</strong> rather than silently picking one: <em>as of March 2026, X</em> is more honest than stating an old fact as current, and it lets the model hedge appropriately. If the same fact is observed again, refresh its recency and nudge confidence up — repeated observation is evidence.</p>",
