                  "<p>With <strong>type-based decay</strong>, because not all facts age at the same rate. <em>Prefers metric units</em> is stable for years; <em>currently debugging the checkout service</em> is stale within a week; a decision and its reasoning stays relevant for a year or so but should be recalled with its date attached. So give each memory type a TTL at write time, set an <code>expiresAt</code>, and filter expired rows out at retrieval so stale facts simply never load. Alongside that, track <code>lastConfirmedAt</code> separately from <code>createdAt</code> and refresh it whenever the fact is observed again — a preference confirmed repeatedly should not expire on the same schedule as one mentioned once. And always inject the as-of date with the fact. Staleness is the failure mode that damages user trust most, because <strong>a confidently-stated wrong fact about the user is far worse than no fact at all</strong> — it makes the whole system feel unreliable in a way a missing detail never does.</p>",
