                solution:
                  "<p>Usually yes, with a carefully worded message. Sending it back lets the model tell the user honestly — <em>I could not access that record; you may need to request access</em> — which is much better than silently failing or producing a vague answer that hides the reason. What you must not do is retry it, since permission does not change between attempts, or let the message suggest a way around it. The wording should be plain and final: state that access is not available and what the user might do about it, without listing what other data exists or hinting at alternative tools that might reach the same information. That last point matters because an agent that keeps probing after a denial is effectively testing your access controls, and the message you send shapes whether it does. Two related notes: the permission check itself must happen in your code against the authenticated session rather than in the model, and repeated denials in one run are worth logging, because a pattern of them can indicate either a misconfiguration or someone probing.</p>",
