                  "<p>From data, not intuition. Run your test set, look at how many steps the <em>successful</em> runs actually take, and set the cap somewhere above the 95th percentile of that — high enough that real work is not cut off, low enough that a wandering run is stopped before it gets expensive. In practice most well-designed agents finish in three to six steps, so a cap in the region of eight to twelve is normal, and a cap of fifty usually means nobody measured. Two things go with it. Steps alone are the wrong single control, so pair the cap with a token budget and a money limit, since cost grows faster than the step count. And treat frequent cap hits as a <strong>diagnosis, not a tuning problem</strong>: if runs regularly reach the limit, the tools are returning unhelpful results, the tool descriptions are ambiguous, or the task needs breaking up. Raising the cap to make the symptom disappear just makes each failure more expensive.</p>",
