                  "<p>Because the cost of a step is not constant. Every step adds the model's reply and the tool result to the conversation, and the whole conversation is re-sent on the next call — so step eight might cost five or ten times what step one did. A run that stays within a ten-step cap can therefore be far more expensive than you assumed, especially if a couple of tools returned large results. Tracking cumulative <strong>tokens</strong> catches that directly. And converting to <strong>actual money</strong> as you go is worth the small effort: it is the number you can explain to a finance team, it keeps working when model prices change or you switch models, and it lets you set a per-run limit like two dollars that means something to the business rather than a token count that does not. The practical setup is to check all of them at the top of each loop iteration and record <em>which</em> one fired, because the mix of stop reasons across your runs tells you where the real problem is.</p>",
