                  "<p>No, and treating them as rivals is a common mistake in interviews. They solve different problems. LangChain is a library of <strong>components</strong> — loaders, wrappers, templates, parsers — and a way to join steps. LangGraph is about <strong>control flow</strong>: how your agent loops, branches, holds state and survives interruption. You can use LangGraph with plain provider SDK calls inside the nodes and never touch a LangChain chain, and plenty of people do exactly that. You can equally use LangChain's loaders with your own hand-written loop. So the real question is not <em>which one</em> but <em>which problems do I actually have</em>: if it is <em>I need to load forty file types</em>, that is a components question; if it is <em>my run has to survive a restart and pause for approval</em>, that is a control-flow question; and if it is neither, the answer is the plain SDK. Framing it that way also makes the third part of the question — when to use neither — fall out naturally rather than sounding contrarian.</p>",
