                solution: `<p>First, I agree what <em>good enough</em> means <strong>before building</strong>, because if you decide afterwards the bar becomes whatever you happened to get. And it is five numbers, not one: a quality bar on a golden set, <strong>safety as pass/fail rather than a percentage</strong> — zero harmful outputs, zero data leaks, one failure blocks the release — a cost per request, p95 latency rather than the average, and the one people miss, <strong>the shape of the failures</strong>. An answer that is obviously wrong is survivable; one that is wrong and sounds confident is not.</p>
             <p>The process is a loop: write the test cases first, build the simplest version, measure to get a baseline, <strong>read thirty or fifty failures by hand</strong>, fix the biggest group, re-measure and check nothing else dropped.</p>
             <p>Three levels: offline as the CI gate, online signals from real traffic, and a human reading a sample every couple of weeks.</p>`,
