                  "<p>Because a real pipeline has stages with different jobs, and one number at one k hides which stage is failing. The <strong>search</strong> stage casts a wide net at k of twenty to fifty, and its job is simply to make sure the answer is somewhere in the pile — measured by recall@50, which is the ceiling for everything after it. The <strong>rerank</strong> stage narrows to five or eight, and its job is to move the best ones to the top — measured by recall@5 and NDCG@5. And what actually <strong>enters the prompt</strong> is measured by precision@5, because there noise costs real tokens. Reporting these together turns a vague complaint into a diagnosis: recall@50 of 0.95 with recall@5 of 0.55 says your search is fine and your ranking is bad, which is a completely different afternoon of work from the reverse. It is also how you check whether a reranker is <em>helping</em> — compare recall@5 before and after it, since rerankers sometimes make things worse on domain content and nobody notices without that comparison.</p>",
