                  "<p>Then it genuinely is a generation problem, and that certainty is exactly what measuring separately bought you — you can now spend effort on the prompt knowing it is the right place. Four things to look at. <strong>The prompt itself</strong>: is it clearly telling the model to answer only from the provided context and to say when the answer is not there? <strong>Context layout</strong>: models attend less to the middle of a long context, so putting the strongest chunks first and last helps, as does labelling each chunk with its source. <strong>Chunk sufficiency</strong>: the retrieval metric said the right chunk came back, but check whether that chunk actually contains enough to answer — this is the most common cause of this exact pattern, and the fix is upstream in chunking or a parent-child strategy rather than in the prompt. And <strong>the model</strong>: a smaller model given good context can still fail to reason across three passages. Before any of that, read thirty failures by hand alongside their retrieved context, because the cause is usually obvious in ten minutes of reading and invisible in the numbers.</p>",
