                solution:
                  "<p>They run in CI alongside the answer-quality eval, because retrieval changes are the ones most likely to break things quietly. Any change to chunking, the embedding model, the index, the search configuration or the reranker should trigger a retrieval run, and I would gate on recall at the final k dropping beyond a small tolerance. Report per category rather than a single average, since chunking changes typically help one type of question and hurt another — long policy documents versus short FAQ entries, for example — and an overall figure hides that trade completely. Two more habits. Track the numbers <strong>over time</strong>, not only per change, because recall drifts downward as the corpus grows and new documents dilute the index, and that decay is invisible without a trend line. And keep the retrieval metrics next to the answer-quality metrics on the same dashboard, so when answers get worse the first question anyone asks is <em>did retrieval change</em> — which is the right question and the one that saves the most time.</p>",
