                  "<p>Ask the question backwards: <strong>generate questions from the answer</strong> and compare them to the real question. If the answer genuinely addresses what was asked, the questions you reverse-generate from it will look very like the original. If it drifted onto a related topic — a correct paragraph about returns when someone asked about refunds — the generated questions will be about returns, and the similarity drops. Take two or three generated questions and average the comparison so one odd generation does not dominate. The reason this technique is worth knowing is that it needs <strong>no ground truth</strong>, which means it works on live production traffic where you have no labels — the same property that makes faithfulness usable in production. Two cautions: it does not catch an answer that is on-topic but wrong, so it must be paired with a correctness check; and a very short answer can score oddly because there is little to generate questions from, so it is worth looking at the two together rather than treating relevance as a standalone verdict.</p>",
