                  "<p>Judge drift is your scores changing when nothing about the product changed — because the judge model was updated by the provider, or someone edited the judge prompt, or the mix of content being graded shifted. It is dangerous because it is completely invisible from the number itself. You see a two-point drop, assume a regression, and spend a day investigating a product that did not change; or worse, you see a rise and ship a change that actually made things worse. The problem is in the ruler, not the thing being measured. Defences: <strong>pin the judge model version</strong> explicitly rather than using a floating alias, treat the judge prompt as versioned code with the same review as anything else, and record which judge version produced every score so you can attribute a shift afterwards. Then <strong>re-validate against the human-labelled set</strong> on any change, plus periodically as a habit. That set is what tells you whether a score move is real, and keeping it is the cheapest insurance in the whole measurement stack.</p>",
