                  "<p>Whenever you are choosing between two versions — two prompts, two models, two retrieval configurations. Asking <em>which of these is better</em> is a much easier task for a model than <em>rate this out of ten</em>, and it sidesteps the whole problem of an unstable absolute scale, so it is the most trustworthy way to use a judge. The catch is position bias, which is why you run both orders and only count agreement. Where pairwise does <em>not</em> help is absolute questions: <em>is this good enough to ship</em>, or <em>did this answer contain the required fact</em>. Those need criteria, because there is no second answer to compare against. So a sensible setup uses both: criterion-based pass/fail as the standing quality gate in CI, and pairwise comparison when a specific decision has to be made between two candidates. One practical note — pairwise results tell you which is better but not by how much, so a version that wins sixty percent of comparisons may still be barely different, and it is worth reporting the win, tie and loss counts rather than a single verdict.</p>",
