                solution:
                  "<p>Three reasons, and none of them are about collecting more feedback. <strong>No context was captured</strong> — you have \"this answer was wrong\" with no record of which chunks were retrieved, which prompt version produced it, or what the automatic checks said, so three weeks later nobody can diagnose it and the item is quietly skipped. <strong>Nothing is clustered</strong> — the raw list is two hundred rows long, it is dispiriting to read, and there is no obvious place to start, so nobody starts. Two hundred complaints are usually five problems, and a list of five causes with counts gets acted on where a list of two hundred items does not. And <strong>nobody owns it</strong> — feedback review is everyone's job in principle and no one's in practice, so it accumulates until someone declares bankruptcy on it. The fix is unglamorous and effective: capture the retrieved chunk IDs and versions with every piece of feedback, cluster by cause automatically, and give one named person thirty minutes a week to look at clusters rather than items.</p>",
