                question:
                  "How long should an online test run?",
                solution:
                  "<p>Long enough to get past <strong>novelty</strong> and to collect enough traffic for the noise to settle — usually at least two weeks, and often longer for AI changes than for a normal product test. Two things drive it. Behaviour immediately after a change is not representative in either direction: some users notice something different and engage more, others are briefly thrown by it, and both effects fade. And AI metrics are noisier because the underlying answers vary, so you need more data to distinguish a real two-point move from run-to-run variation. It is also worth covering at least one full weekly cycle, since traffic mix on a Monday is not the mix on a Saturday and a change can perform differently on each. The discipline that matters most is <strong>not peeking and stopping early</strong> — checking daily and stopping the moment the numbers look good is how you ship noise as a result. Set the duration up front and stick to it, with the exception that a guardrail breach stops the test immediately, since that is a safety valve rather than a result.</p>",
