                  "<p>Everything you did not write but your system depends on. The <strong>model provider</strong> — where your data goes, whether it is used for training, their retention period, and what happens when they deprecate a version. <strong>Open models</strong> pulled from a public hub, which can be tampered with or carry unexpected licence terms. <strong>MCP servers and plugins</strong>, which are the modern version of this problem and the one people forget: connecting a third-party server means running someone else's code that your model can trigger, giving it your credentials, and accepting <em>prompt text</em> from them in the form of tool descriptions. <strong>Fine-tuning datasets</strong> of unclear provenance. And ordinary library dependencies, which are still the most likely thing to bite you. The controls are the familiar ones applied to a new surface: review before adopting, pin versions, check what changes after approval, give each component the narrowest credentials that work, and keep an inventory of what is connected. The MCP-specific twist worth naming is that a server can change its tool definitions <em>after</em> you approved it, so approval is not permanent unless you pin and fingerprint.</p>",
