                  "<p>No, and saying so is worth a lot in a review. It is <strong>additional</strong> to ordinary application security, not a replacement for it. Authentication and authorisation on every endpoint, secrets in a secret manager rather than in a repo, audit logging with sensible retention, patched dependencies, network controls, and a written incident process all still apply — and in practice most real incidents in AI products come from those rather than from exotic model attacks. A team that has memorised the LLM Top 10 while leaving an unauthenticated internal endpoint exposed has not made itself safer. The list is genuinely useful for two things: as a <strong>checklist during a design review</strong>, so nothing AI-specific gets forgotten, and as <strong>shared vocabulary</strong> with a security team, since saying \"excessive agency\" or \"indirect prompt injection\" means something precise to them and saves a long explanation. Treat it as a prompt for the conversation rather than as the whole scope of the work.</p>",
