                solution:
                  "<p>They overlap heavily and use the same mechanism, but the target is different. A <strong>jailbreak</strong> aims at the model's own safety training — getting it to produce content the provider trained it to refuse, regardless of whose application it is running in. <strong>Prompt injection</strong> aims at <em>your</em> instructions — getting it to ignore the rules you set for your product, such as staying on topic, only using retrieved documents, or not revealing certain information. The techniques are largely shared: roleplay, hypothetical framing, claiming a special mode, encoding, and multi-turn build-up all work against both. The distinction matters for who owns the fix. Jailbreak resistance is mostly the model provider's job and improves with each version, so your main jobs are output filtering and not building products whose safety depends on the model refusing. Injection resistance is <em>yours</em>, and no provider improvement removes it, because your application's rules are not part of the model's training. In an interview it is worth naming both and being clear which one you can actually influence.</p>",
