                  "<p>This is the genuinely hard part, and it is how most real leaks happen — not clever attacks, but someone changing teams and the index not knowing yet. Your vector store holds a <em>copy</em> of the ACLs as they were at indexing time, while people join and leave, documents get restricted, and folders get moved every day. Three options. <strong>Scheduled re-indexing</strong> is simple and leaves a window where you are wrong. <strong>Checking every document against the source at query time</strong> is always correct and far too slow across a whole index. The practical answer is the <strong>hybrid</strong>: filter roughly using the permission metadata in the index, rerank, and then verify only the five to ten chunks that survived against the source system before showing them. That is a handful of cheap permission checks rather than thousands, and it is correct. It also gives you free repair — when a verification fails you know that chunk's metadata is stale, so queue it for re-indexing immediately and track the rate as a health metric.</p>",
