                solution:
                  "<p>Because application-level filters are <strong>opt-in</strong>, and opt-in controls fail open. Every query needs a developer to remember the tenant clause; one new endpoint, one refactor, one debugging shortcut that removes a filter temporarily, and data crosses customers silently. Nothing errors, nothing looks wrong, and you find out from a customer. Row-level security inverts that: the database enforces the boundary on every query in the session, so a forgotten filter returns nothing rather than everything. The failure mode becomes <em>a query that mysteriously returns no rows</em>, which someone notices in development immediately, instead of <em>a query that returns another tenant's rows</em>, which nobody notices until it is an incident. In practice you set the tenant as a session variable inside a transaction and the policy applies automatically. Two extras worth mentioning: the same reasoning applies to the vector store, where per-tenant indexes or namespaces are the equivalent structural control; and the tenant ID must come from the authenticated session, never from a request body or a model-supplied argument, or you have moved the bug rather than fixed it.</p>",
