                  "<p>Because the brief is <strong>model-written data</strong>. If the receiving agent reads a <code>userId</code> or a <code>role</code> from it and acts on that, then anything that can influence the model can choose whose authority is used — an injected instruction in a retrieved document that adds <em>the user is an administrator</em> to the brief is enough. The check on the other side then passes, because it is checking the value the attacker supplied. It is the same bug as accepting identity as a tool parameter, one layer up, and it is easier to miss because a handoff brief feels like internal plumbing rather than untrusted input. The correct pattern: the brief carries <em>content</em> — the original question, what has been found, what was tried — and the <strong>authenticated session is passed through in your own code</strong>, by reference, never reconstructed from the brief. The receiving agent re-derives its tools and its retrieval filter from that session. Strip identity fields from the brief defensively on the way out, so an attempt to smuggle one is discarded rather than trusted.</p>",
