                  "<p>Three things. <strong>Time</strong>: ordinary web requests complete in milliseconds, so identity never has a chance to go stale within one. Agent runs pause for approval and resume hours or days later, which introduces a whole class of problem — expired tokens, revoked access, departed users — that request-scoped thinking does not cover. <strong>Breadth</strong>: a normal endpoint serves one resource, so the route-level check is meaningful; an assistant has effectively one endpoint that can reach everything, so authorisation has to move down into the query and the tool executor. <strong>Delegation</strong>: the system takes actions on the user's behalf, sometimes through sub-agents and third-party servers, so identity has to <em>propagate</em> rather than just being checked once — and every hop is a place it can be dropped or, worse, upgraded. There is a fourth if you count it: much of the plumbing carries model-generated data, so identity travelling inside that data is attacker-influenceable in a way that a session cookie is not. Naming these is what shows you have built one rather than reasoned about it from web-app experience.</p>",
