                solution:
                  "<p>Because the same model with the same documents produces three completely different systems depending on the answer. If the consequence is <em>someone notices and asks again</em>, you build a simple pipeline, ship it, measure it and iterate — basic evaluation and a feedback button. If the consequence is <em>we send a customer incorrect pricing</em>, you need citations so claims are checkable, faithfulness checks, a human approval gate on anything binding, and an audit trail. If the consequence is <em>someone is denied a benefit they were entitled to</em>, then a human makes the decision and the system assists, you need full audit logging including the retrieved text, and the decision itself probably belongs in a rules engine with the model only used for reading documents. Notice that nothing about the model changed across those three — everything that differs is architecture, and all of it follows from one sentence from the business. That is why the question has to come before any design work, and why answering it late means rebuilding rather than adding.</p>",
