                  "<p>Take it as a signal that nobody has thought about failure yet, and work through it rather than arguing. Three moves. First, ask about the <strong>current error rate</strong> — the existing process is not perfect either, and putting a number on it reframes the conversation from perfection to improvement. Second, ask what <strong>one wrong answer actually costs</strong>, which is the real question hiding underneath and usually reveals that some errors are trivial and a specific few are serious. Third, separate the two kinds of failure: being wrong, and being wrong <em>without saying so</em>. Almost always the honest requirement is <em>it must never be confidently wrong</em>, which is achievable — the system says it could not find the answer, cites its sources, and escalates when unsure. That reframing usually satisfies the concern completely, because what people fear is not imperfection but silent, plausible errors. From there you can agree a real quality bar plus a defined fallback, and write both down. If they still insist on perfection after that, the honest answer is that this is not a task for a language model, and saying so is better than promising it.</p>",
