                question:
                  "What has to be true about caching in a gateway?",
                solution: `<p>It has to be <strong>permission-aware</strong>, or the cache becomes an access control bypass across your whole organisation — which is much worse than the same bug inside one service.</p>
             <p>The key must include the tenant and the caller's permission groups, not just the prompt. Otherwise one tenant's answer, built from their documents, is served to another tenant who asked a similar question, and the retrieval filtering that was working perfectly is bypassed entirely because the second request never reaches it.</p>
             <p>Two further points. <strong>Exact-match caching is safe; semantic caching needs more care</strong>, since fuzzy matching makes it easier to serve an answer built for a different context. And <strong>caching interacts with attribution</strong> — a cached response costs nothing, so record it as a cache hit rather than as spend, or your per-team costs are wrong in a confusing direction.</p>
             <p>Cache hit rate is also one of the more useful gateway metrics, because it is often the cheapest available cost reduction.</p>`,
