                solution: `<p>The rule: <strong>synchronous only if a person is waiting and it finishes in seconds</strong> — and even then, stream it. Everything else is a job: ingestion always, embedding backfills, re-indexing, batch enrichment, long agent runs, evaluation runs, and anything with human approval, which is async by definition because it may wait hours.</p>
             <p>Four things are different about queues for AI work: <strong>jobs cost money</strong>, so a retry storm is a bill; <strong>jobs are long</strong>, so visibility timeouts must be generous; <strong>poison messages are expensive</strong>; and the big one — <strong>the rate limit is somebody else's</strong>, so concurrency must be capped globally rather than per worker.</p>
             <p>For long jobs: checkpoint after every step, lease with a heartbeat, cap recovery attempts, make side effects idempotent, and give the user a run ID with real progress.</p>`,
