                solution:
                  "<p>Count recovery attempts <em>on the job record</em> and stop after about three, marking it failed and raising an alert. Without that cap, a poison job crashes the worker, the lease expires, recovery picks it up, it crashes again — and that loop runs all night at full speed. In AI pipelines it is worse than usual for two reasons: each attempt may have <em>spent money</em> before crashing, and a crashing worker takes down whatever else it was processing. So the counter must live on the durable job or run record rather than in the queue's redelivery count alone, because a worker that dies hard may never acknowledge the message. Alongside the cap, two things help. Include the <strong>amount already spent</strong> in the alert, because that is what tells you whether this is urgent. And capture enough context to reproduce it — which document, which step, which input — since poison jobs are usually a specific malformed input rather than a general bug, and finding that one file is the fix. Then the same input should become a test case.</p>",
