                question:
                  "What exactly is the N+1 problem?",
                solution:
                  "<p>One query to fetch a list, then one more <strong>per row</strong> to fetch something related. 100 rows means 101 queries.</p><pre><code class=\"language-typescript\">const orders = await repo.find();                    // 1\nfor (const o of orders) {\n  o.user = await users.findOneBy({ id: o.userId });   // N\n}</code></pre><p>Each individual query is fast, so nothing looks wrong. The cost is <strong>round trips</strong> &mdash; at 2ms each, 500 rows is a full second of waiting, and it scales with data volume you do not control.</p><p>It is the most common performance bug in ORM code precisely because the failing version looks like ordinary, readable code.</p>",
